{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"The target's claim that verisimilitude functions as a 'quasi-probabilistic weight' conflates Popper's logical concept of truth-content with the psychological or pragmatic act of preference. Popper explicitly argued that preferring a better-corroborated theory is a rational decision based on its survival of severe tests (its informational content and resilience), not an inductive inference that it is more likely to be true. The target fails to distinguish between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the methodological rule for action (which relies on corroboration without assigning probability), thereby attacking a strawman of Popper's position rather than demonstrating an internal collapse.","target":"42d3d5be8c91fada9027ca691d3cd91048305a27cb99b614b7e58c96c8a3e192"}]}